Hello!
the clover problem was an easy fix :) you just forgot to load the jquery that was needed, and that you use in the script. 
I've added this line 
<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.1.min.js"></script>
before your js script, now it works. Probably this one was a little hidden on codepen and thats why you didn't see it. Usually you can see if they've used jquery if you click on the settingsicon on top of the code window.

Now that we have to think about offline you would have to download this jquery bit 
1. Download jQuery
Open this link in your browser:
https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.7.1.min.js
<!-- Right-click → Save As… -->
Save the file as:
jquery-3.7.1.min.js

2. Put it in your project folder
Example structure:
project/
│
├── index.html
├── plant.html
├── js/
│   └── jquery-3.7.1.min.js
└── images/
(You can also put it next to the HTML file — /js/ is just cleaner.)

3. Link it locally in your HTML
Inside <body> before your own script:
<script src="js/jquery-3.7.1.min.js"></script>
<script>
  // your existing JS here
</script>



then a few more errors on the plant.html 

-> Invalid / problematic content usage
you use 
.hover-img {
  content: url("images/plant/plantpicker1.png");
}
.hover-img:hover {
  content: url("images/plant/plantpicker2.png");
}
content is meant for ::before / ::after, not regular elements.
Some browsers allow this, others don’t → fragile.
Better options:
- Use background-image (I would say this is the easiest one)
- Or swap <img src> with JS
- Or use ::before

solution 1:
.hover-img {
  background-image: url("images/plant/plantpicker1.png");
  background-size: contain;
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
}
.hover-img:hover {
  background-image: url("images/plant/plantpicker2.png");
}
<!-- (Then remove the <img> and make it a div) -->
            <div class="hover-img" width= 80%><img src="images/plant/plantpicker1.png"></div>
<div class="hover-img" src="images/plant/plantpicker1.png"></div>


-> 3. Missing viewport meta (important!)
Without this, mobile layout will behave strangely, but its always good practice to include it even if you don't go for a mobile solution. Probably you will need that in some more files.
Add inside <head>:
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

Things that technically work, but are wrong / risky
-> 4. Invalid image widths
<img src="..." width= 230vw>
width attribute does not accept vw
This is invalid HTML.
Use CSS instead:
<img src="..." style="width:230vw;">

or better in css file or style tags:
.step2 img { width: 230vw; }

-> 5. Unclosed tags
You have this:
<p><b><span class="highlight-white">
✨You’ll get organic, one-of-a-kind botanical prints 
with soft, natural textures- <br> — each piece unique 🌸🌿

Missing closing tags for:
</span>
</b>
</p>
Browsers will guess, but it can mess up layout.

Fix:
<p><b><span class="highlight-white">
✨You’ll get organic, one-of-a-kind botanical prints 
with soft, natural textures — <br> each piece unique 🌸🌿
</span></b></p>

-> 6. overflow: scroll
.tutorial {
  overflow: scroll;
}

This forces scrollbars even when not needed on some browsers.
Better:
overflow: auto;



The Problem with the draggable images --> 
this is a classic positioning clash 
Your layout is trying to use flexbox, but position: absolute is overriding it.
Why they’re stacking on top of each other
This line is the culprit:
.draggable {
  position: absolute;
}
Absolutely positioned elements are removed from the flex layout, so all five images default to the same top-left point of .ring. Flexbox can’t space them if they’re absolute.

Simple, clean solution (absolute only)
Keep your CSS almost the same
.interact {
  position: relative;
  top: 26vh;
  left: 52%;
  width: 30%;
}

.ring {
  position: relative;
  height: 10vw; /* gives a reference height */
}

.draggable {
  position: absolute;
  width: 5vw;
  cursor: grab;
  user-select: none;
  -webkit-user-drag: none;
}

Set different starting positions (CSS)
The easiest way: :nth-child() this is how you can adress different iterations of one item

.ring .draggable:nth-child(1) {
  left: 0;
  top: 0;
}
.ring .draggable:nth-child(2) {
  left: 6vw;
  top: 0;
}
.ring .draggable:nth-child(3) {
  left: 12vw;
  top: 0;
}
.ring .draggable:nth-child(4) {
  left: 18vw;
  top: 0;
}
.ring .draggable:nth-child(5) {
  left: 24vw;
  top: 0;
}

They now appear lined up next to each other, but are still fully draggable.


